        "math_academy_id": "q-31669",
        "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{log}_{5}\u2061(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        "worked_solution": "Let $u={log}_{5}\u2061(5x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\cdot 5=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\,\u27f9\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u20615\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{log}_{5}\u2061(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{1}^{5}{log}_{5}\u2061(5x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u20615\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}\u20615{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}\u20615\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}\u20615\\cdot (\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
